index e696c23e..dd3f109f 100644
--- a/MAINTAINER-README.txt
+++ b/MAINTAINER-README.txt
@@ -249,6 +249,28 @@ verification. Source fetches and sibling implementation checkouts are never
 build inputs. Android, Linux ARM64 and osx-arm64 recipes are described in
 native/host/README.md, LINUX_ARM64.md and MACOS_ARM64.md.
 
+Reusing a copied build tree (lane worktrees seeded with another checkout's
+native/work/cycles-build, prototype/work/cycles-build or
+native/work/android/<rid>/host/build, together with the generated overlay and
+dependency prefix next to them): pass --rebase-build-tree to tools/build.py,
+native/tools/build_cycles.py, prototype/tools/build_host.py or
+native/tools/build_cycles_android.py (tools/rebase_build_tree.py does the work).
+It reads the old checkout root from CMakeCache.txt, moves the object folders
+CMake names after absolute source paths, rewrites the old root in every text
+file of the tree and in the referenced generated inputs under work/ (keeping
+their modification times), gives every referenced source, header, CMake file
+and library the modification time of the same file in the old checkout when
+the two are byte-identical (the old checkout must still exist; it is read
+only), then configures and builds as usual and checks that no build file still
+names the old root and that every compiled source is under this checkout.
+Files that differ from the old checkout rebuild, with everything that depends
+on them; an unchanged tree rebuilds nothing and produces byte-identical
+binaries. A copy that was already stale in the old checkout (objects older than
+their inputs there) rebuilds exactly what the old checkout would have rebuilt.
+Relinked binaries never match the old checkout byte for byte: CMake's build
+RUNPATH names the dependency prefix of the checkout that linked them. The
+overlay generator writes generated includes only when their content changes.
+
 The .NET solution:
 
     dotnet restore CodeBrix.Graphics3D.Cycles.slnx
@@ -343,6 +365,21 @@ After a WGSL change, dump the modules a gate compiles
 (CODEBRIX_CYCLES_WGSL_DUMP=DIR) and run native/tools/intel_compile_envelope.py
 on them: every entry point must stay below 60,000 backend instructions and
 registers (native/DEVICE.md, "Linux reference fixes on the updated Mesa").
+The product carries the same limit as a warning: every pipeline build measures
+the inlined WGSL size of each entry point it compiles (pipelines.h, size guard)
+and warns at 1,200,000 bytes (about 60,000 backend instructions at the measured
+central density of 20 bytes per instruction; 16.9 to 22.1 measured on Mesa
+25.0.7). The warning goes to stderr before the compile, into the device
+report's "warnings" (so ICapabilityReport.Warnings) with a "sizeGuard" object
+(threshold, largest entry, estimate), and into the process report of the gates.
+It never changes the variant, the tier or the "degraded" flag.
+CODEBRIX_CYCLES_PIPELINE_SIZE_WARN_BYTES overrides the threshold (0 disables).
+native/tools/pipeline_size_guard_check.py is its Linux gate check (a render gate
+run at the default and at a lowered threshold; films must be bit-identical).
+The specialization feature mask (pipelines.h ShaderFeatures) is a fixed array of
+64-bit words (256 bits); bit numbers keep their meaning once assigned (0-61 and
+63 are in use, 62 and 64 onwards are free); reports and logs carry feature
+names, never the numeric mask, so widening it changes no persisted format.
